1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,200
Neutrinos occasionally interact with matter.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:07,200
When they do, other elementary particles can be produced.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:11,000
The particles of particular interest here are electrons and muons.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,500
The electron is the familiar negatively charged particle.

5
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:20,200
A muon is similar to an electron, but about 207 times heavier.

6
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:27,600
Muons are especially useful for detecting neutrinos.

7
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:36,000
At high energies, they can travel much farther through matter than electrons, leaving long tracks.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:41,500
Imagine firing projectiles through a sequence of metal plates.

9
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:46,000
The plates represent atoms in the surrounding material.

10
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,500
A small 9 mm projectile represents the electron.

11
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:54,000
In this analogy, it passes through the first few plates,

12
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,200
but changes direction as it goes,

13
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:00,200
knocking fragments from the plates.

14
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:06,200
The fragments make it difficult to identify the original projectile and trace where it came from.

15
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:12,500
Now imagine a much larger projectile, such as a .50-caliber round.

16
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:18,700
In our simplified analogy, it follows a straighter path through more plates.

17
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:22,000
That is our muon.
